The offline compiler compiles system calls for the generic ABI (FEX 3bec2ac, on top of 0014)

On Windows the offline compiler gave its syscall handler a Linux ABI
(OS_LINUX32 for 32-bit images). The WoW64 module's handler uses
OS_GENERIC: the handler reads and writes the guest registers itself.
With a Linux ABI the compiled `int 0x2e` writes the handler's return
value into EAX after the call. Once the system call blocks came from
the caches (0013), that clobbered EAX after every system call and
32-bit ntdll soon stored through a bad pointer.

The compiler now uses OS_GENERIC, like the WoW64 and ARM64EC modules.

diff --git a/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp b/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp
index 71495be..d3de310 100644
--- a/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp
+++ b/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp
@@ -559,7 +559,9 @@ static std::optional<std::string> GenerateSingleCache(FEXCore::ExecutableFileInf
 #ifdef _WIN32
   OvercommitTracker = std::make_unique<FEX::Windows::OvercommitTracker>(IsWine);
 
-  auto SyscallOSABI = Is64Bit ? FEXCore::HLE::SyscallOSABI::OS_LINUX64 : FEXCore::HLE::SyscallOSABI::OS_LINUX32;
+  // WinPad: the WoW64 and ARM64EC modules' syscall handlers use the generic ABI, so a cached
+  // `int 0x2e` must not write the handler's return value into EAX as the Linux ABIs do.
+  auto SyscallOSABI = FEXCore::HLE::SyscallOSABI::OS_GENERIC;
   auto SyscallHandler = std::make_unique<AOTSyscallHandler>(*CTX, SyscallOSABI);
 
   SyscallHandler->VAFileStart =
